# =============================================================================
# Orovion web (Next.js) — environment template
#
#   cp env.example.new .env.example      # replaces the old template
#   cp .env.example .env.local           # then fill in the values
#
# All NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined at BUILD time (`npm run build`), NOT read
# at runtime — set them before building (locally in .env.local, on Vercel in the
# project's Environment Variables) and REDEPLOY after any change.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1) BACKEND
# =============================================================================
# ONE backend: the EC2 box behind Caddy, on its own subdomains.
#
# The old dual-deployment setup (AWS + Render, /health probing, automatic
# failover) has been REMOVED from src/lib/backend.ts. The *_AWS and *_RENDER
# variables are no longer read by anything — delete them from Vercel. Leaving
# them set is worse than removing them: the app silently falls back to
# same-origin and every call is proxied through Vercel (see the warning below).
#
# Two modes:
#
#   PRODUCTION · DIRECT (set the two vars below)
#     The browser calls https://api.orovion.com and https://chat.orovion.com
#     directly. Caddy terminates TLS and upgrades WebSockets natively.
#
#   LOCAL DEV · SAME-ORIGIN PROXY (leave both UNSET, or set them to "proxy")
#     Every /api and /socket.io call goes to this app's own origin and
#     next.config.mjs forwards it to the *_PROXY_TARGET vars below.

# ----- PRODUCTION (direct) -----
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=https://api.orovion.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCKET_URL=https://chat.orovion.com
# Optional — only if call signaling ever moves off the main socket origin.
# Defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCKET_URL.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAT_SOCKET_URL=https://chat.orovion.com

# ⚠️ NEVER route Socket.IO through Vercel (i.e. do not leave SOCKET_URL unset
# in production). Vercel rewrites cannot perform the WebSocket upgrade, so the
# wss:// connection fails outright and the long-polling fallback 400s through
# the serverless proxy. Sockets MUST point straight at chat.orovion.com.

# ----- LOCAL DEV (server-side only; not NEXT_PUBLIC) -----
# Used only when the API base above is unset or set to the literal "proxy".
# BACKEND_PROXY_TARGET=http://localhost:5000
# CHAT_PROXY_TARGET=http://localhost:5001

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKEND CORS — both origins, or login breaks
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# api-service and chat-service both match the Origin header EXACTLY against
# their FRONTEND_URL list. `https://orovion.com` and `https://www.orovion.com`
# are DIFFERENT origins, so the backend .env needs BOTH:
#
#   FRONTEND_URL=https://orovion.com,https://www.orovion.com
#
# Then recreate the containers (`docker compose up -d api chat`) — a plain
# `restart` does NOT re-read env_file.
#
# The refresh cookie is set on .orovion.com (COOKIE_DOMAIN in the backend .env),
# with the leading dot, so it is shared across orovion.com, www.orovion.com and
# api.orovion.com.


# =============================================================================
# 2) SITE IDENTITY / SEO
# =============================================================================
# Canonical origin, used by src/lib/seo.ts for canonical URLs, Open Graph tags
# and the sitemap. Code defaults to https://www.orovion.com when unset.
#
# This must be the SAME host you actually serve — if you later redirect www to
# the apex (or the other way), change it here too, or every canonical tag and
# OG URL points at the redirecting host.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.orovion.com

# Google Search Console verification token. Omit entirely if unused — the
# meta tag is only emitted when this is set.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=


# =============================================================================
# 3) ADMIN CONSOLE (server-only; NOT NEXT_PUBLIC)
# =============================================================================
# The operator console is reachable ONLY at /<ADMIN_PANEL_SLUG>; the literal
# /admin path 404s. Use a long, non-obvious, per-deployment value so the real
# URL never ships in the client bundle and can't be guessed.
# NOTE: middleware runs on the Edge runtime, so Next inlines this at BUILD
# time — it must be present when you run `npm run build`, not only at runtime.
ADMIN_PANEL_SLUG=change-me-to-a-long-random-slug


# =============================================================================
# 4) WEBRTC CALLS (optional)
# =============================================================================
# TURN server — required for calls to connect across different networks/NAT.
# Without it, calls only work between peers that can reach each other directly.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_URL=turn:turn.example.com:3478
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_URL_TLS=turns:turn.example.com:5349
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_USERNAME=user
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_PASSWORD=pass


# =============================================================================
# 5) FIREBASE WEB CONFIG (public values from the Firebase console)
# =============================================================================
# Firebase console -> Project settings -> Your apps -> Web app -> Config.
#
# TWO things that silently break Google sign-in:
#   1. NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID must be the SAME project as the service
#      account key mounted into api-service (/app/firebase-key.json). A mismatch
#      makes verifyIdToken reject every token, surfacing as a generic 401
#      "Invalid or expired Google token".
#   2. Every origin you serve from must be listed under
#      Authentication -> Settings -> Authorized domains — including
#      www.orovion.com, not just orovion.com.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=your-firebase-api-key
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-project.firebaseapp.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project-id
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your-project.appspot.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=000000000000
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:000000000000:web:abcdef0123456789
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX


# =============================================================================
# 6) MICROSOFT CLARITY — heatmaps & session recordings (optional)
# =============================================================================
# clarity.microsoft.com -> your project -> Settings -> Overview -> Project ID.
# It is a short lowercase alphanumeric string (e.g. "abcd1234ef"), NOT a URL and
# NOT a secret — it ships in the client bundle by design. Leave it blank and the
# tag is never injected at all (same pattern as the Google verification token
# above), so local dev and preview builds stay untracked.
#
# Like every NEXT_PUBLIC_* value this is inlined at BUILD time, so setting it in
# Vercel is not enough on its own — you must REDEPLOY before any data appears.
# Heatmaps also need real traffic on a deployed build; nothing shows up from
# localhost unless you deliberately set this in .env.local.
#
# SCOPE — deliberately restricted, see src/lib/clarity.ts:
#   Records:  /  /login  /help  /privacy  /terms  /mobile-app  /team  /team/*
#   Never:    /app/*  /onboarding  /admin (+ the ADMIN_PANEL_SLUG route)
# Session replay captures the DOM, and /app/* renders private clinician DMs,
# case studies, prescriptions and consult notes. The route list is an ALLOWLIST,
# so a newly added route is untracked until it is explicitly added there.
#
# The tag boots cookie-less: it issues clarity("consentv2", ...) with both
# storage types denied, so no _clck/_clsk cookies are set and no cookie banner is
# required today. If you add a consent banner later, grant them there.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARITY_PROJECT_ID=
